<section class="ls-manifesto-chapter" id="continuity">
<header class="ls-chapter-heading ls-container">
<p class="ls-eyebrow">01 / The conviction</p>
<h2>GOOD WORK DESERVES A <span class="ls-stroke">NEXT CHAPTER.</span>
</h2>
</header>
<div class="ls-story-unit" data-tone="paper" data-watermark="planes">
<div class="ls-container">
<div class="ls-principle-pair">
<article class="ls-principle" data-principle="true" id="work-returns">
<span class="ls-principle-index">01 /</span>
<h3>THE WORK RETURNS.</h3>
<p>You finish a brief, then turn to the idea it unlocked. By the time you return, the facts have moved. Your curiosity should not have to pay the full cost of starting again. Let the document carry a method for becoming useful again.</p>
</article>
<article class="ls-principle" data-principle="true" id="method-travels">
<span class="ls-principle-index">02 /</span>
<h3>LET THE METHOD TRAVEL.</h3>
<p>The researcher gathers sources. The builder needs a plan. The writer needs the argument behind it. A working folder can keep those threads close: the document, its recurring jobs and its review history. Give different parts of your work a place to meet.</p>
</article>
</div>
