<section aria-labelledby="why-heading" class="ls-manifesto-why" id="why-living-documents">
<div class="ls-container">
<div class="ls-why-heading">
<p class="ls-eyebrow">WHY THIS NEEDS TO CHANGE</p>
<h2 id="why-heading">AN ANSWER IS ONLY<br/>
<span class="ls-stroke">THE BEGINNING.</span>
</h2>
<p>Someone still has to verify it, connect it to sources and return when the facts change. The next generation of tools has to carry more of that continuing work.</p>
</div>
<div class="ls-why-items">
<article class="ls-why-item">
<div>
<span class="ls-why-source-date">01 / THE VERIFICATION GAP</span>
<h3>More output. Who checks it?</h3>
<p>In Microsoft's survey, 60% said they trusted AI output enough not to check its accuracy.</p>
<p class="ls-why-implication">A polished draft can pass the checking work to someone else. Flow should keep the source, exact change and review decision close enough to inspect together.</p>
<a href="https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/microsoftvivablog/research-drop-fighting-ai-slop-with-meta-cognition-around-ai/4493933" rel="noreferrer" target="_blank">
<span>Microsoft People Science · 2026-02-10<br/>Research Drop: Fighting AI Slop with Meta-Cognition around AI</span>
<svg aria-hidden="true" class="lucide lucide-arrow-up-right" fill="none" height="16" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
<path d="M7 7h10v10">
</path>
<path d="M7 17 17 7">
</path>
</svg>
</a>
<details>
<summary>Study scope and limits</summary>
<p>Self-reported attitudes, not observed error rates. The underlying sample was mid-to-senior business roles at organizations with 1,000+ employees, not a representative prosumer sample. Correlations do not establish causality.</p>
</details>
</div>
<div class="ls-why-stat">
<strong>60%</strong>
<span>felt accuracy checks were unnecessary</span>
<small>2026 analysis of a 2025 survey; 1,800 employees across nine markets.</small>
</div>
</article>
<article class="ls-why-item">
<div>
<span class="ls-why-source-date">02 / THE VALUE GAP</span>
<h3>Faster is easy to feel. Value is harder to prove.</h3>
<p>349 technical workers reported a median 3× speed gain, compared with 1.4–2× gains in the value of their work. METR cautions that perceived gains can exceed actual gains.</p>
<p class="ls-why-implication">Producing more is an incomplete measure of progress. Flow should leave a visible result, revision history and review trail so you can judge what was useful.</p>
<a href="https://metr.org/blog/2026-05-11-ai-usage-survey/" rel="noreferrer" target="_blank">
<span>METR · 2026-05-11<br/>Measuring the Self-Reported Impact of Early-2026 AI on Technical Worker Productivity</span>
<svg aria-hidden="true" class="lucide lucide-arrow-up-right" fill="none" height="16" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
<path d="M7 7h10v10">
</path>
<path d="M7 17 17 7">
</path>
</svg>
</a>
<details>
<summary>Study scope and limits</summary>
<p>Self-reported counterfactual estimates from a selected sample, not measured productivity or a randomized trial. Different questions produce the range of value estimates. This is not evidence that current AI makes all workers slower.</p>
</details>
</div>
<div class="ls-why-stat">
<strong>
<span class="ls-why-metric-part">3×<small>speed</small>
</span>
<span class="ls-why-metric-part">1.4–2×<small>value</small>
</span>
</strong>
<span>self-reported speed / value</span>
<small>Survey, February–April 2026; technical workers, researchers and founders.</small>
</div>
</article>
<article class="ls-why-item">
<div>
<span class="ls-why-source-date">03 / THE DIRECTION GAP</span>
<h3>Delegation still needs direction.</h3>
<p>Across about 400,000 interactive Claude Code sessions, people made roughly 70% of planning decisions while Claude made roughly 80% of execution decisions.</p>
<p class="ls-why-implication">The brief, boundaries and meaning of done still need an owner. Flow should make that intent durable when a recurring job carries work forward.</p>
<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/claude-code-expertise" rel="noreferrer" target="_blank">
<span>Anthropic · 2026-06-16<br/>Agentic coding and persistent returns to expertise</span>
<svg aria-hidden="true" class="lucide lucide-arrow-up-right" fill="none" height="16" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
<path d="M7 7h10v10">
</path>
<path d="M7 17 17 7">
</path>
</svg>
</a>
<details>
<summary>Study scope and limits</summary>
<p>Observational, model-classified transcripts; not causal evidence or direct observation of real-world outcomes. Non-interactive/headless sessions were excluded. Expertise and success classifications have measurement limits. Do not present strict transcript verification rates as product failure rates.</p>
</details>
</div>
<div class="ls-why-stat">
<strong>70%</strong>
<span>of planning decisions made by people</span>
<small>Anthropic analysis of October 2025–April 2026 sessions from about 235,000 users.</small>
</div>
</article>
</div>
<p class="ls-why-closing">A Living Document should carry its context forward, make changes inspectable and keep a routine useful on its next cycle. More output is only a beginning.</p>
<p class="ls-why-context">Research context and our interpretation. These studies did not evaluate Flow. The challenge headlines above are Orionfold’s interpretation; the linked titles are the original publications.</p>
</div>
